    "text": "Here are the relevant news articles:\n\n**Goldman Sachs: High Rates Won't Crash Stocks; Earnings Growth Remains Key**\nAccording to Goldman Sachs, despite the 30-year US Treasury yield surging to 5.3% and the 10-year yield nearing 5% in September 2026, a stock market crash is not inevitable. The firm argues that corporate earnings growth, rather than interest rates, is the core driver of the bull market. While the S&P 500's forward P/E ratio has compressed from 22x at the start of 2026 to 19x, the earnings yield of 5.2% remains 270 basis points higher than the real 10-year Treasury yield of 2.6%, maintaining the relative attractiveness of equities. Historical data cited by Goldman Sachs shows that while the S&P 500 averages a -2% return in the first three months of a rate-hike cycle, it rebounds to an average +9% return over the subsequent 12 months. Goldman Sachs projects S&P 500 earnings per share (EPS) to reach $340 in 2026, a 24% increase year-over-year, and rise to $385 in 2027. This growth is supported by strong corporate balance sheets, with interest coverage ratios in the 99th percentile of the last 20 years. To navigate the high-rate environment, the report advises avoiding interest-rate-sensitive sectors like homebuilders and instead focusing on financial stocks and companies with solid earnings, noting that global M&A volume has already increased by 36% year-to-date as firms invest in AI and streamline operations.\nOriginal language: zh\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 09:03 PM\nSource:[三立新聞](https://www.setn.com/news/1906521)\n\n**US Markets Rebound on Easing Oil Prices and Inflation Data Ahead of Fed Meeting**\nUS stocks rebounded on September 13, 2026, with the S&P 500 climbing 0.9 per cent to 7,656.98 points and the Dow Jones Industrial Average jumping 509 points to 52,573.29, snapping a four-day losing streak. The market rally was driven by easing oil prices, with Brent crude falling 2.8 per cent to $US104.61 after nearing $US110 due to the ongoing war with Iran, and inflation data showing a 3.4 per cent year-over-year increase, which matched economists' expectations. This data strengthened expectations that the Federal Reserve will raise interest rates at its upcoming meeting, pushing the two-year Treasury yield to 4.62 per cent. Federal Reserve Chairman Kevin Warsh maintained a stance against hinting at rate moves, despite President Donald Trump pushing for lower rates. In corporate news, Kroger rose 2.7 per cent on stronger profits, ACV Auctions soared 44.2 per cent following a $US10.50 per share cash offer from Copart, and Oracle finished down 1.7 per cent after an initial 8.5 per cent jump. Meanwhile, the Australian sharemarket futures pointed to a gain of 18 points (0.2 per cent), and the Australian dollar traded at US71.75¢. Consumer sentiment in the US fell, with inflation expectations rising to 4.6 per cent, while European markets rose and Asian markets, including Japan's Nikkei 225 (-1.9 per cent) and South Korea's Kospi (-1.8 per cent), closed weaker.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 07:24 PM\nSource:[Brisbane Times](https://www.brisbanetimes.com.au/business/markets/asx-eyes-gains-wall-street-rises-fed-meeting-in-focus-20260914-p60wzq.html)\n\n**Global Markets Navigate Risks of Inflation, Debt, and Shifting Monetary Policies**\nGlobal stock markets are facing a convergence of economic risks as of September 2026, including persistent inflation, rising oil prices, and record-high global debt levels. According to the International Monetary Fund's reference forecast, global growth is expected to slow from approximately 3.4 percent in 2024-2025 to 3.1 percent in 2026, before rising to 3.2 percent in 2027. Total global government, corporate, household, and financial sector debt reached a record $353 trillion (approximately SR1,323.75 trillion) by the end of the first quarter of 2026, equivalent to roughly three times global gross domestic product. In the US, headline Personal Consumption Expenditures inflation stood at 3.7 percent year on year in July 2026, with core PCE at 3.3 percent, both remaining well above the Federal Reserve's 2 percent target. These conditions could compel the Federal Reserve to raise its federal funds rate above its current target range of 3.50-3.75 percent or maintain rates for longer. Consequently, longer-term US Treasury yields have risen, with the benchmark 10-year yield reaching approximately 4.78 percent and the 30-year yield at 5.25 percent. Japan's monetary policy shift also impacts global markets; the Bank of Japan has raised its policy rate to 1 percent, and the yield on 10-year Japanese government bonds has reached 3 percent. As the largest foreign holder of US Treasury securities, Japan holds approximately $1.12 trillion as of June 2026, and its rising rates may trigger an unwind of the yen carry trade. Despite these pressures, market movements on September 4 remained measured: the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 0.51 percent to close at 53,414.25, the S&P 500 declined 0.38 percent to 7,718.60, and the Nasdaq Composite fell 0.29 percent to 26,506.99. While valuations in AI-related stocks and elevated interest rates present risks, investor confidence remains supported by strong corporate balance sheets and sustained consumer spending. The article concludes that while markets are absorbing pressure, the margin for error is narrowing as central banks attempt to contain inflation without significantly weakening economic growth.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 11:39 AM\nSource:[Arab News](https://www.arabnews.com/opinion/global-stocks-face-a-perfect-storm-of-risks-3001556)\n\n**US Treasury Yields Surge Past 5.3% Amid Structural Shifts and Debt Concerns**\nOn July 31, the 30-year US Treasury yield surpassed 5.27%, its highest level since 2007, and rose above 5.3% in August, while the 10-year yield approached 5%. US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent attempted to lower yields by purchasing $6 billion in bonds, but the effort failed. Meanwhile, Donald Trump has been urging Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh, whom he appointed, to cut interest rates, even asking him to 'get smart.' However, it is highly likely that Warsh and the Fed governors will raise rates next Wednesday to curb inflation. The article attributes the rise in yields to structural forces reversing a 30-year trend, including Baby Boomers drawing down savings instead of accumulating them, a decline in Chinese foreign reserves from over $4,000 billion in 2014 to approximately $3,400 billion, and geopolitical risks such as the war in Iran and frozen Russian assets. Domestically, US federal debt exceeded $40,000 billion by the end of July, with interest payments reaching $857 billion in the first nine months of the fiscal year—a 13% increase from the previous year, surpassing spending on Medicare and defense. Additionally, demand for capital to fund AI data centers is driving up borrowing costs. According to Bloomberg Economics, the natural real rate on 10-year bonds reached 2.6% in the second quarter of 2026, consistent with nominal yields around 4.7%. Jim Reid of Deutsche Bank noted that long-term 10-year yields have averaged 4.5% since 1800, suggesting current levels are a 'normalization.' Consequently, long-term Treasury ETFs, such as those from iShares, have lost 4.4% in total return over the last year, impacting American households through higher mortgage, auto loan, and credit card rates.\nOriginal language: it\nPublish date: September 12, 2026 10:35 PM\nSource:[Il Messaggero](https://www.ilmessaggero.it/economia/news/bond_usa_rendimenti-9759769.html)\n\n**US Stocks Face Critical Test as Fed Rate Hike Expectations Rise Amid High Inflation**\nOn September 11, 2026, the U.S. stock market rebounded with the S&P 500 rising 0.9%, the Dow Jones Industrial Average gaining over 500 points, and the Nasdaq climbing nearly 1%, though all three major indices closed the week lower. The rally was driven by the release of higher-than-expected inflation data, which solidified market expectations that the Federal Reserve will raise interest rates by one basis point this week. According to the article, the U.S. August core Consumer Price Index (CPI) rose 0.3% month-over-month, while overall inflation increased 3.4% year-over-year, significantly exceeding the Fed's 2% target. Following this data, market probability for a rate hike rose to over 80%. The article notes that while investors are relieved the inflation data is not severe enough to alter economic forecasts, the real test for the market's AI-driven rally lies in the Fed's future guidance. RBC strategists now predict three rate hikes by year-end, up from a previous forecast of zero, while federal funds futures indicate a nearly 50% probability that rates will reach 4% to 4.25% by December. A major pressure point remains the bond market, where the U.S. 10-year Treasury yield closed at 4.974% on September 11, nearing the psychological 5% mark for the first time in three years. The article warns that if yields breach 5% alongside oil prices exceeding $100, the AI and chip stocks that have supported the U.S. market this year could face significant challenges.\nOriginal language: zh\nPublish date: September 12, 2026 05:51 PM\nSource:[UDN](https://udn.com/news/story/6811/9750982)\n\n**Fed September 2026 Interest Rate Decision: Date, Time, and Market Impact**\nGlobal markets are closely watching the US Federal Reserve's September 2026 interest rate decision, with investor expectations for a rate hike reaching 90% following recent inflation data. According to the article, the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) meeting is scheduled for September 15-16, 2026, with the interest rate decision to be announced on September 16, 2026, at 21:00 Turkish time. Fed Chairman Kevin Warsh is expected to hold a press conference following the decision to discuss future monetary policy. Recent economic indicators show August consumer prices rose 0.4% monthly and 3.4% annually, while core inflation (excluding food and energy) rose 0.3% monthly but fell to 2.4% annually, the lowest level since March 2021. Despite the drop in core inflation, rising energy prices have bolstered rate hike expectations; specifically, Brent crude oil prices increased 8.5% weekly to reach $104.5 per barrel, and US 10-year Treasury yields approached 5%, currently standing at 4.97%. The article notes that while market pricing suggests a high probability of a rate hike, the final decision remains dependent on the FOMC's evaluation during the meeting. The decision is expected to impact global markets including the dollar, gold, oil, and cryptocurrencies.\nOriginal language: tr\nPublish date: September 12, 2026 09:18 AM\nSource:[birgun.net](https://www.birgun.net/haber/fed-faiz-karari-ne-zaman-aciklanacak-eylul-2026-fed-faiz-karari-734319)\n\n**Goldman Sachs: High Rates Won't Crash Stocks; Earnings Growth Remains Key**\nAccording to Goldman Sachs, despite the 30-year US Treasury yield surging to 5.3% and the 10-year yield nearing 5% in September 2026, a stock market crash is not inevitable. The firm argues that corporate earnings growth, rather than interest rates, is the core driver of the bull market. While the S&P 500's forward P/E ratio has compressed from 22x at the start of 2026 to 19x, the earnings yield of 5.2% remains 270 basis points higher than the real 10-year Treasury yield of 2.6%, maintaining the relative attractiveness of equities. Historical data cited by Goldman Sachs shows that while the S&P 500 averages a -2% return in the first three months of a rate-hike cycle, it rebounds to an average +9% return over the subsequent 12 months. Goldman Sachs projects S&P 500 earnings per share (EPS) to reach $340 in 2026, a 24% increase year-over-year, and rise to $385 in 2027. This growth is supported by strong corporate balance sheets, with interest coverage ratios in the 99th percentile of the last 20 years. To navigate the high-rate environment, the report advises avoiding interest-rate-sensitive sectors like homebuilders and instead focusing on financial stocks and companies with solid earnings, noting that global M&A volume has already increased by 36% year-to-date as firms invest in AI and streamline operations.\nOriginal language: zh\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 09:03 PM\nSource:[三立新聞](https://www.setn.com/news/1906521)\n\n**US Treasury Yields Surge Past 5.3% Amid Structural Shifts and Debt Concerns**\nOn July 31, the 30-year US Treasury yield surpassed 5.27%, its highest level since 2007, and rose above 5.3% in August, while the 10-year yield approached 5%. US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent attempted to lower yields by purchasing $6 billion in bonds, but the effort failed. Meanwhile, Donald Trump has been urging Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh, whom he appointed, to cut interest rates, even asking him to 'get smart.' However, it is highly likely that Warsh and the Fed governors will raise rates next Wednesday to curb inflation. The article attributes the rise in yields to structural forces reversing a 30-year trend, including Baby Boomers drawing down savings instead of accumulating them, a decline in Chinese foreign reserves from over $4,000 billion in 2014 to approximately $3,400 billion, and geopolitical risks such as the war in Iran and frozen Russian assets. Domestically, US federal debt exceeded $40,000 billion by the end of July, with interest payments reaching $857 billion in the first nine months of the fiscal year—a 13% increase from the previous year, surpassing spending on Medicare and defense. Additionally, demand for capital to fund AI data centers is driving up borrowing costs. According to Bloomberg Economics, the natural real rate on 10-year bonds reached 2.6% in the second quarter of 2026, consistent with nominal yields around 4.7%. Jim Reid of Deutsche Bank noted that long-term 10-year yields have averaged 4.5% since 1800, suggesting current levels are a 'normalization.' Consequently, long-term Treasury ETFs, such as those from iShares, have lost 4.4% in total return over the last year, impacting American households through higher mortgage, auto loan, and credit card rates.\nOriginal language: it\nPublish date: September 12, 2026 10:35 PM\nSource:[Il Messaggero](https://www.ilmessaggero.it/economia/news/bond_usa_rendimenti-9759769.html)\n\n**US Treasury Yields Near 4.8%: 5% Threshold Triggers Market Concerns Amid Inflation and Oil Risks**\nOn September 1, the U.S. 10-year Treasury yield reached 4.79% and remained at 4.78% on September 4, approaching the psychological threshold of 5% as investors weighed higher oil prices, inflation risks, and the U.S. fiscal outlook. According to Reuters, renewed U.S.-Iran fighting pushed Brent crude to $94.65 a barrel on September 1, while the Federal Reserve has warned that inflation remains above its 2% target. Federal Reserve Governor Christopher Waller stated on September 3 that inflation was 'meaningfully above' the 2% goal, and stronger-than-expected August inflation could justify a rate increase at the September 15-16 meeting. The Congressional Budget Office projects a $1.9 trillion federal budget deficit in fiscal 2026, equal to 5.8% of GDP, with net interest outlays expected to rise to $2.1 trillion by 2036. Treasury Secretary data indicates the department expects to borrow $739 billion in the July-September quarter and $628 billion in the October-December quarter. Anthony Saglimbene, chief market strategist at Ameriprise Financial, noted that a 5% yield could serve as a 'psychological level' triggering de-risking by traders. Consequently, the average U.S. 30-year fixed mortgage rate reached 6.71% for the week ending September 3, up from 6.66% the previous week. Brent crude rose 1.5% to $97.60 a barrel, its highest level in seven weeks, due to tensions around the Strait of Hormuz.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 07, 2026 06:10 PM\nSource:[International Business Times Singapore](https://www.ibtimes.sg/us-treasury-yields-near-4-8-will-5-trigger-new-market-shock-93468)\n\n**US Stocks Open Flat After Strong Jobs Report Boosts Fed Rate-Hike Bets**\nOn September 4, 2026, Wall Street opened nearly flat as investors digested a surprisingly strong August jobs report that increased expectations for a Federal Reserve rate hike. The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 101.2 points, or 0.19%, to 53,584.89, while the S&P 500 gained 2.5 points, or 0.03%, to 7,750.19, and the Nasdaq Composite added 3.8 points, or 0.01%, to 26,587.896, according to Reuters. The August employment report showed US employers added 162,000 jobs, significantly exceeding the roughly 53,000-56,000 jobs expected by economists, while the unemployment rate remained at 4.1%. This data shift caused market pricing for a 25-basis-point rate hike at the September meeting to rise to around 60% from 55%, according to Reuters, or 58% in other updates. The stronger employment data increased the possibility of tighter monetary policy, pushing Treasury yields higher, with the 2-year Treasury yield reaching its highest level since January 2025. While a resilient economy supports corporate earnings, higher interest rates can increase borrowing costs and reduce the appeal of equities. Investors are now awaiting upcoming inflation data to determine if the Fed will raise rates or maintain the current 3.5%-3.75% target range.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 04, 2026 05:25 PM\nSource:[The Sunday Guardian Live](https://sundayguardianlive.com/business/why-is-us-stock-market-up-today-dow-falls-019-sp-500-edges-003-higher-nasdaq-gains-001-as-jobs-report-lifts-fed-rate-hike-bets-heres-what-investors-need-to-know-277170/)\n\n**U.S. Futures Fall as August Jobs Report Tops Forecasts, Shifting Fed Rate Outlook**\nU.S. equity futures moved lower on Friday, September 04, 2026, after the Labor Department reported that nonfarm payrolls increased by 162,000 in August, significantly exceeding the economist forecast of 55,000. The unemployment rate remained unchanged at 4.1%, contrary to expectations of a rise to 4.2%, while July's figure was revised to a gain of 21,000 jobs from a previously reported decline of 23,000. This stronger-than-expected data shifted investor focus back to the Federal Reserve's interest rate outlook, causing Treasury yields to rise. According to the CME Group's FedWatch Tool, the probability of a quarter-point rate increase in September dropped to 52.4% from 63.2% earlier in the week, following comments by Federal Reserve Governor Christopher Waller who indicated he would be 'inclined to support' holding rates steady if disinflation signs continued. Dan Coatsworth, head of markets at AJ Bell, noted that 'constantly changing interest rate expectations have kept investors on their toes this week.' Prior to this shift, Wall Street had advanced on Thursday, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average climbing 624.16 points (1.2%) to 53,686.11, the Nasdaq Composite rising 366.23 points (1.4%) to 26,584.06, and the S&P 500 gaining 81.11 points (1.1%) to 7,747.71. Snowflake (NYSE:SNOW) saw its stock jump 16.6% on Thursday after reporting fiscal second-quarter results that beat expectations and issuing higher guidance.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 04, 2026 01:11 PM\nSource:[ADVFN UK](https://uk.advfn.com/market-news/article/22351/u-s-futures-turn-lower-as-august-jobs-growth-tops-forecasts-dow-jones-sp-nasdaq-wall-street)\n\n**Stocks Rebound as Treasury Yields Ease and Fed Signals Rate Pause**\nOn September 3, 2026, U.S. stock markets rebounded strongly, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average gaining 624.16 points to close at 53686.11 (1.18% increase) and the Nasdaq Composite rising 366.232 points to 26584.06 (1.40% increase), according to data from Schaeffer's Investment Research. The S&P 500 Index also rose 81.11 points (1.06%) to 7747.71, while the S&P 100 Index gained 43.03 points (1.13%) to 3844.99. This marked the strongest one-day gain for all three major indexes since August 4. The rally was driven by easing Treasury yields, which fell back below 4.8% after Federal Reserve Governor Christopher Waller signaled support for maintaining interest rates at the September meeting, provided inflation remains contained. The Cboe Volatility Index (VIX) declined 0.88 points (5.79%) to 14.32, marking its sixth drop in seven sessions. In commodities, WTI crude oil rose 0.3% to settle at $91.30 per barrel amid ongoing tensions in the Gulf, while gold prices also increased, supported by a pullback in the U.S. dollar and Treasury yields.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 03, 2026 08:25 PM\nSource:[Schaeffer's Investment Research](https://www.schaeffersresearch.com/content/ezines/2026/09/03/dow-jumps-624-points-as-stocks-build-on-rebound)\n\n**Why September Remains Wall Street's Cursed Month Amid $40tn Debt and AI Capital Crunch**\nThe article analyzes why September is historically a weak month for Wall Street, noting that since 1950, the S&P 500 has averaged a -0.68% return with only a 44% probability of gains, making it the only month to finish in the red more often than green. While no single event defines this 'September effect' unlike the October crashes of 1929 or 1987, the article attributes the trend to post-vacation portfolio reassessments, fiscal year-end rebalancing, and a heavy slate of economic releases including Federal Reserve meetings. The core concern for September 2026 is not just the seasonal trend but a convergence of high bond yields and massive debt issuance: US public debt crossed the $40tn threshold on August 19, 2026, with Treasury data showing a $40.08tn figure shortly after. In the July-September 2026 quarter alone, the Treasury projects $739bn in new net marketable issuance. This supply surge, combined with 10-year Treasury yields at 4.7% and 30-year yields topping 5.2%, pressures growth stocks, particularly in the AI sector where giants like Alphabet, Amazon, Meta, and Microsoft are projected to spend up to $770bn in capital in 2026. This competition for capital creates a delicate loop where rising yields increase interest costs—already $1,170bn for the federal government in fiscal 2026 through July—potentially driving a market rotation from speculative growth to profitable, cash-generating firms. Key market-moving dates include the August jobs report on September 4 (following a July loss of 23,000 jobs), the August CPI on September 11, the Federal Reserve meeting on September 15-16 (where the policy rate sits between 3.50% and 3.75%), and the August PCE data on September 30 (with July reading at 3.7%).\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 01, 2026 01:47 PM\nSource:[Market Screener](https://www.marketscreener.com/news/september-wall-street-s-cursed-month-ce7858ddd08ff721)\n\n**Wall Street Braces for Volatile September Amid Record Debt and High Bond Yields**\nAccording to the article 'September, the cursed month of Wall Street' published on September 1, 2026, by Zonebourse, Wall Street is bracing for a volatile September driven by high bond yields and record US debt rather than just the historical 'September effect'. Historically, since 1950, the S&P 500 has averaged a decline of -0.68% in September with a 44% probability of rising, a trend attributed to post-summer portfolio rebalancing and increased economic data releases. However, current market anxiety stems from the US government bond market, where 10-year yields are approximately 4.7% and 30-year yields exceed 5.2%, making risk-free returns attractive compared to equities. This pressure is exacerbated by the US public debt crossing the $40 trillion threshold on August 19, 2026, with the Treasury projecting $739 billion in new net emissions for the July-September 2026 quarter alone. Consequently, interest expenses for the federal government reached approximately $1.17 trillion for the fiscal year 2026 up to July. Simultaneously, technology giants like Alphabet, Amazon, Meta, and Microsoft are competing for capital, with UBS estimating their investment spending could reach $770 billion in 2026. The article highlights that while Q2 earnings were strong, with FactSet anticipating S&P 500 profit growth of over 25% for the remainder of 2026, the high cost of borrowing poses a risk to growth stocks. Key market-moving events include the August employment report on September 4 (following a July loss of 23,000 jobs), the August CPI on September 11, the Federal Reserve meeting on September 15-16 where the target rate sits between 3.50% and 3.75% (with three of twelve members previously voting for a 0.25% hike), and the August PCE inflation data on September 30, which showed a 3.7% annual rate in July.\nOriginal language: fr\nPublish date: September 01, 2026 01:25 PM\nSource:[zonebourse](https://www.zonebourse.com/actualite-bourse/septembre-le-mois-maudit-de-wall-street-ce7858ddd08dff22)\n\n**Treasury Yields Fall as July Jobs Report Dashes September Hike Bets**\nU.S. Treasury yields fell on Friday after July payrolls unexpectedly dropped by 23,000 jobs, compared to a forecast for 80,000 job gains, according to data reported by Reuters. The unemployment rate eased to 4.1% as labor participation fell, and average hourly earnings rose 3.2% on the year, below the consensus 3.5% increase. Tom di Galoma, managing director of global rates trading at Mischler Financial Group, stated that the weak labor market 'takes the Fed off the hiking table.' Consequently, the odds of a Federal Reserve interest-rate hike in September fell to 44% from 55%. The 2-year note yield dropped 4.35 basis points to 4.202%, reaching its lowest since July 17, while the benchmark 10-year note yield fell 1.44 basis points to 4.656%. Despite the weakening jobs data, traders maintain a 77% probability of a rate increase by December. The Treasury Department is scheduled to sell $125 billion in coupon-bearing debt next week, including $58 billion in 3-year notes, $42 billion in 10-year notes, and $25 billion in 30-year bonds. Market volatility is also being influenced by concerns over the Iran conflict and upcoming consumer price inflation data for July.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: August 07, 2026 07:28 PM\nSource:[mint](https://www.livemint.com/market/treasury-yields-fall-as-jobs-report-dashes-hike-bets-11786130890731.html)\n\n"
