   "reasoning": "FINAL: 3.48\n\nHypotheses:\n- H0: FY2026 average interest rate ends near status-quo/slow-drift, about 3.3%–3.6%.\n- H1: Lower outcome, about 3.0%–3.3%.\n- H2: Higher outcome, about 3.6%–4.0%.\n- H3: Much higher, above 4.0%.\n- H4: Much lower, below 3.0%.\n\nEvidence by hypothesis:\n- E1/E2 [F6]: no verified 2026-09-30 value yet. Consistent with all hypotheses; basically no update.\n- E3 [A1]: Treasury methodology is a slow-moving stock-average over total unmatured interest-bearing debt. This supports H0 and disfavors H2/H3/H4 extremes, because the series should move gradually unless rates stay very different for a long time.\n- E4 [A1]: As of 2025-07-31, debt held by the public averaged 3.8% and intragovernmental 3.1%. Since total interest-bearing debt combines these, this points to a total somewhat below 3.8 and above 3.1, plausibly in the mid-3s by then. That supports H0/H2, especially a value somewhat above FY2024’s 3.324 by FY2026.\n- E5 [A1]: Bills and FRNs were high (4.3%/4.4%), notes and bonds lower (~3.1%-3.3%), TIPS very low in this measure. This composition supports continued upward drift as older low-rate debt rolls off, but not a spike, because much of the stock remains in lower-coupon longer maturities. Supports H0 and mildly H2; contradicts H3 somewhat.\n- E6 [A1]: Reminds us not to confuse interest outlays with this average-rate metric. Neutral on level, but reinforces gradualism and stock averaging, again favoring H0 over tails.\n\nWhat must change before resolution:\n- For H1 (<3.3): Treasury borrowing costs would need to have fallen enough, and enough debt refinanced into lower rates, to pull the stock average down by Sep 2026. Possible, but with only a bit over a year from the latest cited July 2025 context, a large decline seems limited.\n- For H2/H3 (>3.6 or >4.0): market rates would need to stay elevated or rise further, and rollover into higher-cost debt would need to dominate. H2 is plausible; H3 needs a stronger and more persistent high-rate environment than current anchors suggest.\n- Because this metric changes slowly, non-status-quo outcomes require sustained rate differences, not brief market moves.\n\nBase rate and update:\n- Blind base rate centered around 3.4%-3.5%, with p50 roughly mid-3.4s.\n- I update slightly upward from the CBO-ish 3.3%-3.4% anchor because the latest composition evidence shows public debt already around 3.8% by mid-2025, implying some continuing pass-through to the total by Sep 2026.\n- But I keep the move modest because total debt includes lower-rate intragovernmental holdings and because stock-average rates adjust slowly. So I move from a blind median around ~3.45 to ~3.48.\n\nForecast:\n- Median: 3.48%\n- Main mass: roughly 3.3% to 3.8%\n- Upside tail above 4.0 exists but is limited; downside below 3.1 also limited absent a fast rate-cut/refinancing effect.\n\n```json\n{\"percentiles\": {\"5\": 3.02, \"10\": 3.11, \"20\": 3.24, \"40\": 3.40, \"60\": 3.56, \"80\": 3.80, \"90\": 4.01, \"95\": 4.18}}\n```",
