  "da_critique": "Strongest red-team case: the team is probably too high, because they appear to be anchoring on the wrong debt concept and on noisy component rates rather than the exact resolving series.\n\n1. Possible resolution-criterion mismatch\nThe resolution is not asking for a market yield, interest expense ratio, or “federal debt held by the public.” It resolves specifically to Treasury Fiscal Data’s “Average Interest Rate Amount” for:\n- Security Type Description: “Interest-bearing Debt”\n- Security Description: “Total Interest-bearing Debt”\nrecorded on or closest to 2026-09-30.\n\nBut the evidence the team cites is mostly about other categories:\n- E4 gives 3.8% for debt held by the public and 3.1% for intragovernmental holdings.\n- E5 gives individual instrument rates like bills at 4.3%, notes at 3.1%, bonds at 3.3%, FRNs at 4.4%, TIPS at 0.9%.\nThose are not the resolving object. Using public-debt or instrument-level rates to infer the “Total Interest-bearing Debt” series can bias upward, because the total includes a large intragovernmental component at lower rates (per E4, 3.1%), and the final total is a weighted average across categories.\n\n2. The best available official signal points lower than the median forecast\nEven from the team’s own evidence, the July 2025 official data imply a lower overall level than their FY2026 median of 3.71%.\n- E4 says debt held by the public averaged 3.8%, intragovernmental averaged 3.1%.\nA weighted average of those two should land somewhere between them, likely materially below 3.8% because intragovernmental debt is a nontrivial share of total interest-bearing debt. That suggests the actual “Total Interest-bearing Debt” series in mid-2025 is probably around the mid-3s, not already near 3.7.\nIf the team’s 2026 median is 3.71, they are implicitly assuming another sizable rise over roughly 14 months. That may be too aggressive given rollover mechanics.\n\n3. They may be overestimating pass-through speed from current issuance rates to the stock average\nAverage interest rate on total debt is a slow-moving stock measure, not a spot measure. E6 explicitly warns that average rates are a measure of borrowing-cost trends across security types, not the same as interest expense. The US debt stock reprices gradually because much of it is locked in older notes and bonds.\nThe team seems to infer from high short-end rates and bill/FRN readings in E5 that the average stock rate will keep rising quickly. But:\n- Bills and FRNs reprice quickly, yet longer-duration notes/bonds dominate a large share of the stock.\n- Notes and bonds in E5 are only 3.1% and 3.3%, not dramatically above the total-stock level one would infer from E4.\n- If policy rates decline or even stabilize, incremental issuance rates could flatten or fall before enough old low-coupon debt rolls off to push the stock average toward 3.9–4.2.\nSo the right critique is not that the average cannot rise, but that the team may be assuming too much/too fast transmission.\n\n4. Base-rate neglect: this series tends to move smoothly, not explosively\nThe debt-average-rate series is path-dependent and smoother than market rates. The forecast’s upper tail to 4.04/4.18 by Sep 2026 looks like it may be importing market-volatility intuition into an accounting stock variable. Given the gradual turnover of outstanding debt, a one-year move of several tenths is possible, but the team’s distribution places a lot of mass above 3.9 despite official category evidence in E4-E5 that does not clearly support such a sharp move in the aggregate total.\n\n5. Evidence quality is thin and mostly contextual\nE1 and E2 explicitly admit the actual 2026-09-30 resolving value is unavailable and even the exact official series could not be located through the search. That means the forecast may rest on extrapolation from partial proxies rather than the precise historical series. In that setting, one should be cautious about a fairly elevated median and especially about confidence in the upper tail. The absence of the exact series is a reason to regress harder toward the most directly inferable aggregate from E4, which again points lower.\n\n6. Overlooked mechanism: composition can keep the total below public-debt rates\nE3 says the Total Interest-bearing Debt average excludes TIPS and FRNs. That matters because E5 shows FRNs at 4.4% and TIPS at 0.9%; excluding them changes composition relative to crude averages from all debt instruments. More importantly, if the team informally mixed high-yielding bills/FRNs/public debt into the total concept, they may have overstated the aggregate. The total series, by definition, is a specific weighted construct, and composition effects can keep it lower than eyeballing from current issuance rates suggests.\n\nBottom line: the forecast likely overstates the FY2026 total-debt average rate because it leans on non-resolving categories (E4, E5), likely overestimates rollover speed (E6), and underweights the lower-rate intragovernmental share visible in the official evidence (E4). The forecast should move down by about 0.2 to 0.4 percentage points, especially the median and upper quantiles."
